 ## Overview
-Multi-Hit BVH Traversal is a technique to determine the $N$ closest intersections of a viewing ray with compound clip objects (geometric primitives organized in a Bounding Volume Hierarchy) to construct clip intervals for volume rendering [S1]. It sets itself apart from all-hit queries by using a preallocated static array to avoid costly dynamic memory reallocation, which is essential for real-time implementation [S1]. Unlike texture-based approaches that require multiple rendering passes or depth peeling, this method calculates intervals directly in on-chip GPU memory [S1].
+Multi-Hit BVH Traversal is a SIMD-friendly clipping algorithm for ray-traced direct volume rendering (DVR) that identifies visible volume regions by intersecting viewing rays with arbitrary geometric surface primitives (planes, quadrics, triangle meshes) organized in a Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) [S1]. It sets itself apart from rasterization-based depth peeling or texture-based slicing approaches by implementing clipping as a simple two-pass algorithm that avoids multiple render passes, dynamic memory reallocation, and the conversion of parametric surfaces to polygon meshes [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** A ray defined by origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$; a set of clip primitives (planes, spheres, triangle meshes) organized in a BVH; an integer $N$ representing the maximum number of intersections to retrieve [S1].
-*   **Output:** An array of $N$ intersection distances (or hit records) sorted by proximity to the ray origin, used to construct clip intervals [S1].
+The method determines the $N$ closest intersections of a viewing ray with compound clip objects to construct clip intervals for volume rendering. It operates in two passes: first, intersecting primary viewing rays with all opaque geometry to identify visible volume regions; second, casting primary rays through the volume density and considering only voxels within those visible regions [S1].
 
-**Data Structures**
-*   **Clip Primitives:** Stored as Plain Old Data (POD) objects in a C array to avoid virtual inheritance and vtable lookups, reducing indirection overhead [S1].
-*   **BVH:** Manages triangle meshes. The BVH is rebuilt completely if the triangle mesh changes, as dynamic updates are not supported in this implementation [S1].
-*   **Result Array:** A static memory array of size $N$ [S1]. The size $N$ must be decided a priori [S1].
+### Inputs and Outputs
+*   **Inputs:**
+    *   Primary viewing rays defined by origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{d}$ [S1].
+    *   Clip geometry primitives (planes, spheres, triangle meshes) stored as Plain Old Data (POD) objects in a C array [S1].
